# 3. INTRODUCTION

Parking management is an important requirement in shopping malls, hospitals, airports, universities, offices, residential communities, railway stations, and smart-city infrastructure. Traditional parking systems often require drivers to manually search for available spaces, which can increase parking search time, traffic congestion, and inefficient utilization of available parking areas.

The proposed Smart Parking System provides an automated method for monitoring parking slots. Ultrasonic sensors are installed for individual parking spaces and continuously measure the distance between the sensor and an object. When a vehicle is present, the measured distance becomes smaller and the corresponding slot is classified as occupied. When no vehicle is present, the measured distance remains above the configured threshold and the slot is classified as free.

An Arduino UNO acts as the central controller. It receives sensor measurements, processes the readings, determines slot status, counts available spaces, and controls the LCD, LEDs, and buzzer.

The project requirements specifically define the workflow as parking slot → ultrasonic sensor → distance measurement → microcontroller → occupied/available decision → available slot count → LCD/LED/buzzer output.

