# 26. CONCLUSION

The Smart Parking System using Ultrasonic Sensors successfully demonstrates an embedded solution for automated parking-slot monitoring. The system uses four HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensors connected to an Arduino UNO to measure parking-slot distances and determine whether each slot is free or occupied.

A configurable 15 cm threshold is used for the prototype. The Arduino processes the sensor readings, calculates the number of available spaces, and displays the result on a 16×2 I2C LCD. A green LED indicates parking availability, while a red LED and buzzer provide a full-capacity alert.

The complete prototype was implemented and tested virtually using Wokwi. Multiple scenarios, including all slots free, partially occupied parking, vehicle departure, and complete parking occupancy, were successfully tested.

The project provides practical experience with ultrasonic sensors, GPIO, embedded C/C++, distance calculation, threshold-based decision making, LCD interfacing, LED control, buzzer control, serial communication, debugging, and virtual embedded-system simulation.

The prototype can serve as a foundation for future development involving ESP32, IoT connectivity, cloud dashboards, mobile applications, RFID, automatic barriers, online reservations, and smart-city parking infrastructure.

